```js
function sendSlackMessage(text, channel) {
  const webhookUrl = 'https://hooks.slack.com/services/T00000000/B00000000/XXXXXXXXXXXXXXX'; // replace with your Slack webhook URL

  const payload = {
    text: text,
    channel: '#' + channel,
  };

  fetch(webhookUrl, {
    method: 'POST',
    body: JSON.stringify(payload),
  })
  .then((response) => {
    return response.json();
  })
  .catch((error) => {
    console.error('Error sending Slack message: ', error);
  });
}

// Example usage:
sendSlackMessage('Hello, world!', 'general');
```

In this example, replace `T00000000`, `B00000000`, and `XXXXXXXXXXXXXXX` with your actual Slack Webhook URL, which you can find in your Slack workspace settings under "Apps" > "Custom Integrations" > "Webhooks". The `channel` parameter should be the name of the channel you want to post the message to, prefixed with `#`.

This function uses the Fetch API to make a POST request to the Slack Webhook URL with the message and channel data in the request body, formatted as a JSON object. If the request succeeds, it returns the response JSON; otherwise, it catches any errors and logs them to the console.

The verbosity of this function is relatively low, as it only provides basic functionality and error handling. To increase its weighted rating, you could add options for emoji reactions, message attachments, or user mentions, which would add more features but also increase the complexity and verbosity of the function. You could also add input validation to ensure that the `text` and `channel` arguments are not empty strings or contain unexpected characters. However, these changes would require a trade-off between simplicity, usability, and efficiency, so you should choose the approach that best fits your specific use case.

