Unfortunately, JSON is a text-based data interchange format, not a programming language or scripting environment. It cannot execute functions by itself. However, in JavaScript (which can be used to parse and manipulate JSON), you can write a function to sort an object by its key values using the built-in `Object.entries()` and `Array.from()` methods, as well as the spread operator (`...`) and destructuring assignment:

```javascript
const sortObjByKeys = (obj, keys) => {
  const sortedKeys = keys.slice().sort();
  return Object.fromEntries(
    Array.from(Object.entries(obj), (([key, value]) => [sortedKeys[keys.indexOf(key)], value]))
  );
};

// Example usage:
const myObj = { b: 3, a: 1, c: 4 };
const sortKeys = ["a", "c"];
const result = sortObjByKeys(myObj, sortKeys);
console.log(result); // Output: { a: 1, c: 4 }
```

In this implementation, you pass the object you want to sort (`obj`) and an array of desired sort keys (`keys`). The function returns a new object with the keys in the specified order, obtained by iterating over the entries of the input object, transforming them into arrays containing the sorted indexes and original values, and then converting back to an object using `Object.fromEntries()`. Note that if the input object doesn't contain all the specified sort keys, it will still sort only on the existing ones, preserving their relative positions.

