To optimize the storage space for this field while still maintaining a high level of accuracy, you can consider using the `numeric` data type instead of `float`. While `float` is suitable for approximate numeric values, `numeric` provides exact arithmetic for decimal numbers. This makes it ideal for storing monetary amounts or other financial data.

Additionally, you can use the `setval` function to initialize the column with a default value that takes up less space. For example:

```sql
ALTER TABLE mytable ALTER COLUMN val TYPE numeric(10,2) USING (val::numeric(10,2));

CREATE FUNCTION init_val() RETURNS numeric(10,2) LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$
DECLARE
    v NUMERIC(10,2) := 0;
BEGIN
    RETURN v;
END;
$$;

ALTER TABLE mytable ALTER COLUMN val SET DEFAULT (init_val)();
```

In this example, we're converting the existing `float` column to `numeric(10,2)`, which has a fixed precision of 10 digits and a scale of 2 decimal places. We're also defining a new function `init_val` that returns the default value of 0 as a `numeric(10,2)`. Finally, we're updating the `SET DEFAULT` statement for the `val` column to use our new function.

This approach will not only save storage space but also improve the query performance by avoiding the need for conversion between `float` and `numeric`. Note that if your application requires more decimal places than specified here, adjust the `numeric` data type accordingly.

