Here's the C code to convert `argv` (command-line arguments) to a null-terminated string:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    size_t arg_len = 0;
    char *args = malloc(1); // Allocate initial space for first argument

    for (int I = 0; I < argc; i++) {
        size_t len = strlen(argv[i]);
        args = realloc(args, arg_len + len + 1); // Resize array to accommodate new argument
        memcpy(args + arg_len, argv[i], len + 1); // Copy argument into array
        arg_len += len; // Update total length of string
    }

    // Null terminate the string
    args[arg_len] = '\0';

    printf("Converted arguments: %s\n", args);

    free(args); // Free memory allocated dynamically

    return 0;
}
```

This program uses dynamic memory allocation with `malloc()` and `realloc()` to concatenate all the command-line arguments together into a single null-terminated string. The `memcpy()` function is used to copy each argument into the growing string, and the `strlen()` function is used to determine the length of each argument. After all the arguments have been processed, the resulting string is printed using `printf()`, and then the memory allocated dynamically is freed using `free()`. This program assumes that the user will not exceed the maximum possible number of arguments (which is implementation-defined). If you want to handle larger inputs, you may need to use an alternative approach such as `getline()` or read the input from a file instead.

