Unfortunately, I am not aware of any built-in React utility that can automatically remove widows (lines of text that appear by themselves at the top of a paragraph). Widow removal is typically handled by adjusting line breaks and spacing within a layout or design system, rather than through a programming library.

However, there are some CSS techniques you can use to minimize widows:

1. Use `orphans` and `widows` properties to set the minimum number of lines for both orphans (the last line of a paragraph on a page) and widows (a single line at the top of a new page/column). For example:

```css
p {
  orphans: 2;
  widows: 2;
}
```

2. Adjust line height and margin to prevent tight spacing between lines and reduce the likelihood of widows. For instance:

```css
body {
  line-height: 1.5;
  margin-bottom: 1rem; /* Increase this value if necessary */
}
