As a best practice, I would recommend following these steps to update your .NET 7 application without replacing the running EXE:

1. Extract the contents of the ZIP file to a new folder on the local machine. Let's call this folder `update`.

2. Copy the updated DLLs and other necessary files from the `update` folder to the existing installation directory (where the current EXE is located). Make sure to preserve the directory structure so that the application can find the required dependencies.

3. Stop any running instances of the application. This is important because we don't want to update the running process, as it could cause issues or crashes.

4. Create a new .NET Core Host Builder configuration for the updated application. In the `Program.cs` file, create a new `CreateHostBuilder()` method that loads the updated assembly:

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration;
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;

namespace MyApp
{
    public class Program
    {
        public static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            CreateHostBuilder().Build().Run();
        }

        public static IHostBuilder CreateHostBuilder()
        {
            var configuration = ConfigurationManager.GetConfiguration();

            return Host.CreateDefaultBuilder(args)
                .ConfigureAppConfiguration((hostingContext, config) =>
                {
                    config.AddJsonFile("appsettings.json", optional: false, reloadOnChange: true);
                    config.AddEnvironmentVariables();
                })
                .ConfigureServices((context, services) =>
                {
                    // Register services here
                })
                .UseContentRoot(Directory.GetCurrentDirectory())
                .UseStartup<Startup>()
                .ConfigureWebHostDefaults(webBuilder =>
                {
                    webBuilder.UseUrls("http://localhost:5000");
                });
        }
    }
}
```

Notice that we're using the `UseContentRoot()` method to set the content root to the current directory, where the updated application is located. We're also registering our services and creating the WebHost

