```javascript
const redis = require('redis');

class RedisClient {
  constructor() {
    this.client = new redis();
    this.client.on('error', (err) => {
      console.error('Redis Client Error: ', err);
    });
  }

  isAlive() {
    return this.client.ping() !== null;
  }

  async get(key) {
    return await this.client.get(key);
  }

  async set(key, value, duration) {
    return await this.client.setex(key, duration, value);
  }

  async del(key) {
    return await this.client.del(key);
  }
}

const redisClient = new RedisClient();
module.exports = redisClient;
```

In this implementation, we first import the `redis` package to interact with Redis. Then, we define the `RedisClient` class with a constructor that initializes a new Redis client and listens for errors using the `on('error')` method.

The `isAlive` method checks whether the Redis connection is alive by calling the `ping()` method and returning `null` if there's no response from Redis.

The `get`, `set`, and `del` methods are all asynchronous functions that take a string `key` as an argument and perform their respective operations on the Redis server. The `set` method also accepts an optional `duration` argument which specifies the time-to-live (TTL) for the key in seconds.

Finally, we create and export an instance of `RedisClient` called `redisClient`. This instance can be imported and used throughout your application to interact with Redis.

