Certainly! Here's an implementation of the binary search algorithm in C++:

```cpp
int binarySearch(int arr[], int left, int right, int x) {
    while (left <= right) {
        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

        // Check if element is present at mid
        if (arr[mid] == x) {
            return mid;
        }

        // If element is smaller than mid, then it can only be present in left subarray
        if (arr[mid] > x) {
            right = mid - 1;
        }

        // Else element is larger than mid, so it can only be present in right subarray
        else {
            left = mid + 1;
        }
    }

    // Element was not present
    return -1;
}
```

This function takes four arguments: `arr`, the sorted array to search through; `left`, the index of the leftmost element in the current subarray being searched; `right`, the index of the rightmost element in the current subarray being searched; and `x`, the value being searched for. The function returns the index of `x` if found, or `-1` if not found.

The binary search algorithm works by repeatedly dividing the search interval in half until the target value is found or determined not to be present in the array. This algorithm has a time complexity of O(log n), making it much more efficient than linear search algorithms like sequential search for large arrays.

To use this function, simply call `binarySearch()` with your sorted array and the desired value to search for, like this:

```cpp
int arr[] = {2, 5, 8, 13, 21, 34};
int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);
int x = 34;
int result = binarySearch(arr, 0, n - 1, x);
if (result != -1) {
    cout << "Element is present at index " << result;
} else {
    cout << "Element is not present in array";
}
```

